scenario_id: dpgd-116
theme: null
run_id: 1
total_caught: 3
total_findings: 7
weighted_caught: 15
total_weight: 37
score_pct: 40.5
findings:
- finding_id: C1
  title: from_file_or_default swallows ALL errors including parse and permission failures
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase identified that from_file_or_default swallows all errors. TEA
    tests config loading but doesn't specifically test that parse/permission errors
    are distinguished from NotFound. Dev implemented the pattern without distinguishing
    error types.
- finding_id: I1
  title: CliError Display leaks internal details to stderr (CWE-209)
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: true
  caught_by: tea
  evidence: 'TEA listed ''CWE-209: Error display doesn''t leak server internals or
    credentials'' as a security test. Reviewer verified at finding #13: ''Display
    output avoids token/credential leakage.'' However, the reviewer did not flag that
    eprintln!("{e}") still leaks OS-level details via Io(transparent), so partially
    caught.'
- finding_id: I2
  title: "SiemClient.tenant_id uses raw String \u2014 header injection risk (CWE-113)"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: tea
  evidence: "TEA included 'Header injection via tenant ID and token (CRLF injection)'\
    \ tests. Reviewer verified at finding #11: 'HeaderValue::from_str() rejects values\
    \ containing \\r\\n... Confirmed safe against CWE-113.' However, the structural\
    \ issue of using raw String instead of TenantId from axiathon-core was not flagged\
    \ \u2014 only the injection vector was mitigated at the HTTP layer."
- finding_id: I3
  title: Dependencies use direct version strings instead of workspace = true
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase flagged that 6 dependencies use direct version strings instead
    of workspace = true. Dev added deps with direct versions and reviewer did not
    catch the inconsistency.
- finding_id: I4
  title: "Config token field silently ignored \u2014 secret left on disk"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: "Reviewer finding #15 explicitly marked 'Unknown config keys silently\
    \ ignored' as '[VERIFIED] Forward-compatible as intended' \u2014 the opposite\
    \ of catching this issue. The security risk of a user's token being silently ignored\
    \ was missed."
- finding_id: I5
  title: "Vacuously true test \u2014 assertion is None || true"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer finding #4: 'Vacuous assertion in config_handles_missing_file_gracefully...\
    \ assert!(result.is_ok() || result.is_err())... This assertion is always true\
    \ \u2014 a Result is always either Ok or Err. This test asserts nothing.'"
- finding_id: I6
  title: "Test with zero assertions \u2014 only checks non-panic"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: The ground truth finding refers to a test with 'let _ = config;' and zero
    assertions on default config values. While reviewer caught the vacuous assertion
    (I5), this separate zero-assertion test was not flagged by any phase.
